<BaseLayout title="About · David Silva" description="Software engineer working on AI systems in production. What I work on and how I think.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I am a software engineer. I started in full-stack work, .NET and Angular,
    building the kind of systems a business actually runs on: a CMS, a hotel
    booking engine wired to a dozen external APIs, a channel manager keeping
    reservations in sync. Over time the work pulled toward AI. First came
    retrieval-augmented chatbots that answer from a product's own docs, at
    Omnibees through HSystem, and then, at SAP through Zallpy, multi-agent systems
    running in production, where dozens of agents cooperate inside a state machine
    and the hard part is rarely the model and almost always getting the pieces to
    behave together.
  </p>

  <p>
    I came to this through a longer path than a job title shows. I like problems
    where the mess is the point: ambiguous requirements, systems that have to keep
    running while you change them, decisions made under constraints rather than in
    a textbook. The work I am proud of is less about a clever trick and more about
    turning something tangled into something another person can pick up and use.
  </p>

  <p>
    That instinct did not start with code. Before engineering was my whole job I
    taught computing to classes of mixed ages, and I hold a teaching degree. The
    habit stuck: an idea only counts once someone else can act on it. That is still
    how I judge a design, by whether a teammate can read it, extend it, and trust
    it without me in the room. A lot of what I write here comes from trying to
    explain, clearly, things I had to learn the hard way.
  </p>

  <p>
    Away from the keyboard, I have been the dungeon master of the same tabletop RPG
    campaign for a couple of years. It is a hobby, but not an idle one: keeping a
    world coherent across dozens of sessions, with its own threads, characters, and
    rules reacting in real time, turns out to exercise the same muscles good
    software does.
  </p>

  <p>
    The thread across all of it is the same: I like taking complexity and giving it
    a shape clear enough to hand to someone else. This site is where I write that
    down.
  </p>

